# The Event Sourcing Paradigm

At this point we have a barebones Phoenix project, which has a room schema and CRUD pages.  A room has an id, name and description.  The Phoenix generators created some basic CRUD pages which allow us to create, update and delete rooms using the HTML forms provided.  Now what?

Let's take a step back and examine the hard problem we're trying solve.  Each client/player (I will use client and player interchangeably) will visit the "show" path for the room `/rooms/:id` and expect to "see" something.  They will also expect to "see" each other.  And when players manipulate the environment (pick up and throw an object, kill a monster, open a door, pickup a key), we expect other players to see those changes in their own respective browsers.  So the big problem definition we have is a syncronization problem between the clients.  How do we provide all clients with the current state of the world, take input changes from all clients, and syncronize those changes back out to each client?

This sounds like a job for event sourcing.

Event sourcing is simply recording all events as facts in a cronological ledger.  This historical record of all the facts is a "stream" of data called the event stream.  All clients are constantly producing events, just by entering the room and moving around.  So all clients are event producers.  From the event stream we can build "projections" or alternate representations of the state of the world.  In fact, each client is also consumer of all events that all clients have created and will create a projection of the state of the world.  Each client can be thought of as rendering an independent simulation of their world and the only thing keeping all the clients in sync is the data that is streamed to them.  


## Defining events schema

Our events will have an event name and a payload for the event.  

Here are some examples of events that look like they could be published by a client:

```javascript
"item_picked_up", { "user" => "bob", "item" => "cup5" }
"user_pressed_switch", { "user" => "tom", "switch" => "door_trigger" }
"user_moved", { "user" => "bob", "position" => [1,2,3] }
```

This seems pretty flexible and straight forward.  It even tells a story and if we read from beginning to end we can replay an entire scene.

It will also be useful to be able to order events (we'll add tiemstamps) and also a sequence number in case two events arrive at the same time.  Finally all events belong to to a room so we'll have a room_id.  But all of these events can be added on the server side.

## Send events somewhere to be saved

To publish events within the server side, we can just use Phoenix PubSub.  

```elixir
PubSub.broadcast(Xr.PubSub, "room_stream:123", {"user_moved", %{"user_id" => "tom", "pose" => ...}})
```
In this example "stream:123" is the name of a topic.  And to subscribe and receive events from a particular topic we can just do this in a process:

```elixir
PubSub.subscribe(Xr.PubSub, "room_stream:123")
```

Let's create a GenServer that will receive messages on a `stream:#{room_id}` topic and just temporarily
 store the events in the GenServer memory for now. 


Create a new folder at `lib/xr/servers` where we'll put our genserver code.

At a new file there called `event_dispatcher.ex` and paste the following:

```elixir
defmodule Xr.Servers.EventDispatcher do
  use GenServer
  alias Phoenix.PubSub

  def start_link(room_id) do
    GenServer.start_link(__MODULE__, {:ok, room_id})
  end

  def init({:ok, room_id}) do
    # subscribe to the room stream
    PubSub.subscribe(Xr.PubSub, "room_stream:#{room_id}")

    {:ok, %{room_id: room_id, events: [], next_sequence: 0}}
  end

  # responds to incoming message from the room stream
  def handle_info({event_name, payload}, state) do
    state = %{
      state
      | next_sequence: state.next_sequence + 1,
        events: [
          %{sequence: state.next_sequence, event_name: event_name, payload: payload}
          | state.events
        ]
    }

    {:noreply, state}
  end
end

```

Here is a corresponding test that illustrates that this GenServer stashes events into its state right now:

```elixir
defmodule Xr.Servers.EventDispatcherTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: true

  alias Xr.Servers.EventDispatcher
  alias Phoenix.PubSub

  test "receives and stores events in its state" do
    room_id = "abc"

    # Start the EventDispatcher GenServer
    {:ok, pid} = EventDispatcher.start_link(room_id)

    # Broadcast an event
    Phoenix.PubSub.broadcast(
      Xr.PubSub,
      "room_stream:#{room_id}",
      {"user_moved", %{"user_id" => "tom2", "position" => [1, 2, 3]}}
    )

    # Broadcast another event
    Phoenix.PubSub.broadcast(
      Xr.PubSub,
      "room_stream:#{room_id}",
      {"user_moved", %{"user_id" => "bob", "position" => [11, 12, 13]}}
    )

    # Allow some time for the message to be processed
    :timer.sleep(100)

    # Fetch the state of the GenServer
    state = :sys.get_state(pid)

    assert state.room_id == room_id
    assert state.next_sequence == 2
    assert length(state.events) == 2

    event = hd(state.events)
    assert event.sequence == 1
    assert event.event_name == "user_moved"
    assert event.payload == %{"user_id" => "bob", "position" => [11, 12, 13]}
  end
end
```


## Create the Events table

Saving the events into the GenServer is going to make us run out of memory, so we'll need to flush that cache to a database table that will serve as a journal for all the events that happen in a single room.  The table will need a uuid for each event, a sequence id to order the event, timestamps, a room_id to associate all the events belonging to a room, an event name and a payload for the event.

This generator will create an events table and add it under the rooms context.  It will also create some basic HTML CRUD pages, but we won't end up using most of them.

```bash
mix phx.gen.html Rooms Event events room_id:references:rooms sequence:integer event_name:string payload:map --binary-id
```

Make the following changes to the migration file:

```elixir
defmodule Xr.Repo.Migrations.CreateEvents do
  use Ecto.Migration

  def change do
    create table(:events, primary_key: false) do
      add :id, :binary_id, primary_key: true
      add :sequence, :integer, null: false, default: 0
      add :event_name, :string, null: false
      add :payload, :map, default: %{}
      add :room_id, references(:rooms, on_delete: :delete_all, type: :string)

      timestamps(type: :utc_datetime)
    end

    create index(:events, [:room_id])
  end
end

```

Make the following changes to the schema file:

```elixir
defmodule Xr.Rooms.Event do
  use Ecto.Schema
  import Ecto.Changeset

  @primary_key {:id, :binary_id, autogenerate: true}
  @foreign_key_type :binary_id
  schema "events" do
    field :payload, :map
    field :sequence, :integer
    field :event_name, :string
    field :room_id, :string

    timestamps(type: :utc_datetime)
  end

  @doc false
  def changeset(event, attrs) do
    event
    |> cast(attrs, [:sequence, :event_name, :payload])
    |> validate_required([:sequence, :event_name])
  end
end

```

Run the migration:

```bash
mix ecto.migrate
```

## Modify GenServer to batch save 

Now we have a table.  Let's modify the GenServer to periodically stash the events into the events table.  The generator that we ran earlier created some functions in the rooms context.  Most of them we do not need. 

Edit the rooms.ex context file and remove the update and delete event functions so that you're left with this:

```elixir
  alias Xr.Rooms.Event

  @doc """
  Returns the list of events.

  ## Examples

      iex> list_events()
      [%Event{}, ...]

  """
  def list_room_events(room_id) do
    Repo.all(from e in Event, where: e.room_id == ^room_id, order_by: e.sequence)
  end

  @doc """
  Creates a event.

  ## Examples

      iex> create_event(%{field: value})
      {:ok, %Event{}}

      iex> create_event(%{field: bad_value})
      {:error, %Ecto.Changeset{}}

  """
  def create_event(attrs \\ %{}) do
    %Event{}
    |> Event.changeset(attrs)
    |> Repo.insert()
  end
  ```

When we list events it only makes sense to do so in the context of a room, so we replaced the list_events function with a list_room_events function that takes a room_id.  
Be sure to also remove all the event tests from the rooms test file and fix the function name in the EventController.

```elixir
defmodule XrWeb.EventController do
  use XrWeb, :controller

  alias Xr.Rooms

  def index(conn, %{"room_id" => room_id}) do
    events = Rooms.list_room_events(room_id)
    render(conn, :index, events: events)
  end
end

```

